17:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1e",null,{"guide":{"date":"December 25, 1861–early March 1862","place":"Rolla, Lebanon, and Springfield, Missouri, to Fayetteville and the Boston Mountains in northwest Arkansas","result":"Samuel Curtis drove Sterling Price and the Missouri State Guard out of Missouri. Curtis then kept the Army of the Southwest in northwest Arkansas, where Earl Van Dorn prepared to attack it with the combined forces of Price and Ben McCulloch","facts":[{"label":"United States force at Lebanon","value":"Just over 12,000 men and 50 artillery pieces"},{"label":"Price at Springfield","value":"About 7,000 men"},{"label":"Route of advance","value":"The Telegraph Road from Rolla through Springfield into northwest Arkansas"},{"label":"Distance Price retreated","value":"About 100 miles from Springfield into the Boston Mountains"},{"label":"Curtis’s supply line","value":"Nearly 200 miles back to the railhead at Rolla"},{"label":"Confederate advantage after concentrating","value":"About 7,000 more men than Curtis, according to the episode"}],"maps":[{"title":"The campaign region today","caption":"Rolla and Lebanon are in central Missouri. The Telegraph Road continued southwest through Springfield, crossed the Arkansas line, and ran through Cross Hollow and Fayetteville toward the Boston Mountains.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-95.1%2C35.1%2C-90.4%2C38.5&layer=mapnik&marker=36.454%2C-94.034"},{"title":"Curtis pursued Price into Arkansas","caption":"Blue shows the Army of the Southwest advancing from Rolla. Red shows Price retreating from Springfield to the camps near Fayetteville and Strickler Station. The diagram follows the route described in the episode.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/planyourvisit/the-pea-ridge-campaign.htm","image":"/images/pea-ridge-approach-map.svg","alt":"Schematic campaign map showing Curtis advancing south from Rolla and Price retreating from Springfield into northwest Arkansas"},{"title":"The Pea Ridge campaign","caption":"The National Park Service campaign map places the movement in the larger Trans-Mississippi theater. It marks Rolla, Lebanon, Springfield, Fayetteville, Pea Ridge, the Telegraph Road, and the roads and rivers connecting them.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/planyourvisit/the-pea-ridge-campaign.htm","image":"/images/pea-ridge-campaign-map.jpg","alt":"National Park Service map of the Pea Ridge campaign in Missouri and Arkansas"},{"title":"Where the campaign was heading","caption":"A U.S. Army Corps of Engineers map of the Pea Ridge battlefield. The battle itself is covered in the following episodes; this map identifies the roads, settlements, and terrain toward which both armies were moving.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99447263/","image":"/images/pea-ridge-battlefield-map.jpg","alt":"1876 map of the Pea Ridge battlefield showing roads, settlements, terrain, and troop positions"}],"moments":[{"time":"3:28","audioTime":208,"title":"The episode defines why Pea Ridge matters","detail":"Pea Ridge was fought in northwest Arkansas on March 7–8, 1862. The Confederate army outnumbered the United States army, included a substantial contingent of American Indian troops, and forced Curtis to reverse the direction of his defensive line."},{"time":"5:12","audioTime":312,"title":"Price and McCulloch remained divided after Wilson’s Creek","detail":"After their August 1861 victory at Wilson’s Creek, Sterling Price took the Missouri State Guard north to Lexington while Ben McCulloch returned with Confederate troops to northwest Arkansas."},{"time":"5:47","audioTime":347,"title":"Price could not hold Lexington","detail":"A strong United States column advanced from St. Louis after Price captured Lexington. Price withdrew to Springfield in southwest Missouri."},{"time":"6:01","audioTime":361,"title":"A pro-Confederate Missouri government voted to secede","detail":"Claiborne Fox Jackson assembled a rump legislature at Neosho. Its secession ordinance was disputed, but the Confederate government recognized Missouri and added a star for the state to its flag."},{"time":"6:34","audioTime":394,"title":"Jefferson Davis created a new command above Price and McCulloch","detail":"The Confederate government formed the Trans-Mississippi District and appointed Major General Earl Van Dorn to direct both commanders."},{"time":"7:44","audioTime":464,"title":"The episode introduces Earl Van Dorn","detail":"Van Dorn graduated from West Point in 1842, served and was wounded in Mexico and on the Texas frontier, resigned from the U.S. Army after Mississippi seceded, and reached major general in the Confederate army in September 1861."},{"time":"9:51","audioTime":591,"title":"Van Dorn took command at Little Rock","detail":"Van Dorn arrived at Little Rock on January 29, 1862. He worked to make Price and McCulloch cooperate and planned an offensive into Missouri."},{"time":"10:27","audioTime":627,"title":"Van Dorn initially planned a March 20 offensive","detail":"He told Price that reinforcements would create a force of at least 15,000 men. Van Dorn hoped to defeat United States forces in Missouri and eventually capture St. Louis."},{"time":"10:50","audioTime":650,"title":"Price’s retreat changed the plan","detail":"On February 22, Van Dorn learned that Price had abandoned Springfield ten days earlier and retreated to northwest Arkansas. Price’s camp was now close to McCulloch’s winter quarters."},{"time":"12:23","audioTime":743,"title":"Halleck wanted Price removed from Missouri","detail":"Major General Henry Halleck took command of the Department of the Missouri in November 1861. Driving out Price would let Halleck transfer garrison troops to operations on the Mississippi, Tennessee, and Cumberland rivers."},{"time":"13:46","audioTime":826,"title":"Curtis received the southwest Missouri assignment","detail":"On December 25, Halleck placed Brigadier General Samuel R. Curtis in command of the new District of Southwest Missouri and ordered him to defeat, disperse, or destroy Price’s force."},{"time":"14:38","audioTime":878,"title":"The episode introduces Samuel Curtis","detail":"Curtis graduated from West Point in 1831, became a lawyer and engineer, served in the Mexican War, represented Iowa in Congress, organized Iowa troops in 1861, and became a brigadier general in August."},{"time":"18:32","audioTime":1112,"title":"Curtis established his headquarters at Rolla","detail":"Rolla was the railhead and the starting point for an advance into southwest Missouri. Franz Sigel objected to serving under Curtis, briefly resigned, and then returned as second in command."},{"time":"21:31","audioTime":1291,"title":"Halleck ordered Curtis forward","detail":"On January 13, 1862, Curtis began pressuring Price. He advanced from Rolla to Lebanon along the Telegraph Road and organized the Army of the Southwest there."},{"time":"21:51","audioTime":1311,"title":"Curtis organized four divisions","detail":"Peter Osterhaus commanded the 1st Division and Alexander Asboth the 2nd under Franz Sigel. Jefferson C. Davis commanded the 3rd Division and Eugene Carr the 4th under Curtis’s direct control."},{"time":"22:25","audioTime":1345,"title":"The Army of the Southwest concentrated at Lebanon","detail":"Curtis had just over 12,000 men and 50 artillery pieces. Captain Philip Sheridan managed the long supply line back to Rolla."},{"time":"23:17","audioTime":1397,"title":"Curtis began the final advance on Springfield","detail":"The army left Lebanon on February 10. Price had only about 7,000 men and could not expect to hold Springfield against a determined advance."},{"time":"24:03","audioTime":1443,"title":"Price abandoned Springfield","detail":"Curtis reached within eight miles of Springfield by February 12. Price left that night and began a roughly 100-mile retreat toward the Boston Mountains in northwest Arkansas."},{"time":"25:17","audioTime":1517,"title":"The armies fought at Little Sugar Creek","detail":"On February 17, Confederate troops took a blocking position at James Dunnegan’s farm on the Telegraph Road. After the first Civil War engagement fought in Arkansas, the rear guard withdrew safely."},{"time":"25:49","audioTime":1549,"title":"Price and McCulloch agreed to withdraw farther south","detail":"Price initially wanted to make a stand. News that United States cavalry had raided Bentonville convinced him to accept McCulloch’s recommendation to continue the retreat."},{"time":"26:23","audioTime":1583,"title":"Fayetteville was looted and burned during the retreat","detail":"Military stores were opened as the Confederates passed through. Soldiers looted military and civilian property, and fires set in buildings used by the army spread through much of the town."},{"time":"27:21","audioTime":1641,"title":"The Confederate forces concentrated near Strickler Station","detail":"Price’s Missourians reached the winter camps south of Fayetteville in the Boston Mountains. The retreat placed them close to McCulloch’s troops."},{"time":"27:35","audioTime":1655,"title":"Curtis occupied Cross Hollow and reconnoitered Fayetteville","detail":"Curtis took the abandoned camps at Cross Hollow and sent 1,200 men down the Telegraph Road. They reached Fayetteville on February 23 and stayed for two days."},{"time":"28:00","audioTime":1680,"title":"Curtis chose to remain in Arkansas","detail":"Curtis had expelled Price from Missouri, but his army was eight miles inside Arkansas at the end of a supply line nearly 200 miles long. He kept the position despite facing a larger combined Confederate force."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"blue","branches":[{"commander":"Henry W. Halleck","command":"Department of the Missouri","subordinates":"Directed the Missouri and river campaigns"},{"commander":"Samuel R. Curtis","command":"District of Southwest Missouri; Army of the Southwest","subordinates":"Overall field command"},{"commander":"Franz Sigel","command":"1st and 2nd Divisions","subordinates":"Peter J. Osterhaus and Alexander Asboth"},{"commander":"Jefferson C. Davis","command":"3rd Division","subordinates":"Under Curtis’s direct control"},{"commander":"Eugene A. Carr","command":"4th Division","subordinates":"Under Curtis’s direct control"},{"commander":"Philip H. Sheridan","command":"Quartermaster","subordinates":"Managed the supply line from Rolla"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"red","branches":[{"commander":"Jefferson Davis","command":"Confederate president","subordinates":"Created the Trans-Mississippi District"},{"commander":"Earl Van Dorn","command":"Trans-Mississippi District","subordinates":"Overall command of Price and McCulloch"},{"commander":"Sterling Price","command":"Missouri State Guard","subordinates":"Retreated from Springfield into Arkansas"},{"commander":"Benjamin McCulloch","command":"Confederate forces in northwest Arkansas","subordinates":"Wintered near Cross Hollow and Strickler Station"}]}],"people":[{"name":"Samuel R. Curtis","role":"Organized and led the Army of the Southwest from Rolla into northwest Arkansas","side":"United States","image":"/images/samuel-r-curtis.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018669095/"},{"name":"Earl Van Dorn","role":"Took command of the Trans-Mississippi District and concentrated Price’s and McCulloch’s forces","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/earl-van-dorn.jpg","source":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/learn/historyculture/general-van-dorn.htm"},{"name":"Franz Sigel","role":"Curtis’s second in command and commander of the Army of the Southwest’s 1st and 2nd Divisions","side":"United States","image":"/images/franz-sigel.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666489/"},{"name":"Henry W. Halleck","role":"Department commander who ordered Curtis to drive Price out of Missouri","side":"United States"},{"name":"Sterling Price","role":"Led the Missouri State Guard from Springfield into northwest Arkansas","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Benjamin McCulloch","role":"Commanded Confederate troops wintering in northwest Arkansas and argued for withdrawing south","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Claiborne Fox Jackson","role":"Pro-Confederate Missouri governor who assembled the rump legislature at Neosho","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Philip H. Sheridan","role":"Served as the quartermaster responsible for Curtis’s long supply line","side":"United States"}],"losses":[],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/pea-ridge-battle-print.jpg","alt":"1889 chromolithograph of United States and Confederate troops fighting at Pea Ridge","caption":"Kurz & Allison published this popular print of the Battle of Pea Ridge in 1889. It is a later artistic interpretation, not an eyewitness image.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2013645343/"},{"image":"/images/pea-ridge-campaign-map.jpg","alt":"National Park Service campaign map showing the route from Rolla to Pea Ridge","caption":"The campaign map shows the long route connecting Curtis’s railhead at Rolla to the Confederate camps in northwest Arkansas.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/planyourvisit/the-pea-ridge-campaign.htm"}],"sources":[{"label":"Civil War Podcast episode 101","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.libsyn.com/101-pea-ridge-part-the-first"},{"label":"National Park Service: The Pea Ridge Campaign","url":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/planyourvisit/the-pea-ridge-campaign.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Samuel R. Curtis","url":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/learn/historyculture/curtis.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Earl Van Dorn","url":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/learn/historyculture/general-van-dorn.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Pea Ridge order of battle","url":"https://www.nps.gov/peri/learn/historyculture/order-of-battle.htm"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Pea Ridge battlefield map","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99447263/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Samuel R. Curtis portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018669095/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Battle of Pea Ridge print","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2013645343/"}]}}]}]
